ITEMS=[]
def Q(stem,key,d1,d2,d3,why):ITEMS.append((stem,[key,d1,d2,d3],why))
Q("A firearm is stolen but the licence card is safe. Which reporting duty applies?",
 "Report the theft to police within 24 hours","Report the lost card to the Registrar","Notify the Registrar within 30 days","Report it at the next licence renewal",
 "Section 120(11) requires reporting the firearm's theft within 24 hours after becoming aware of it, to the police station nearest to where it occurred.")
Q("A licence card is stolen but the firearm is secure. Which report is required?",
 "Tell the Registrar within 24 hours","Report a firearm theft to the police","Nothing, since the firearm is safe","Apply to renew the licence early",
 "Section 29 requires the holder to notify the Registrar of the loss or theft of the licence document within 24 hours of discovery.")
Q("A person knows where unlawfully held cartridges are hidden. What does section 120(2) require?",
 "Tell police where they are at once","Take them home and hand them in later","Report them to police within 30 days","Report only firearms, not ammunition",
 "Section 120(2) requires reporting the location to a police official without delay. It does not authorise taking possession.")
Q("Information in an approved application changes. What must the holder do?",
 "Notify the Registrar within 30 days","Report to police within 24 hours","Wait until the renewal","Notify the DFO within 7 days",
 "Section 26 requires written notification of changed application information within 30 days.")
Q("Under section 120(11), when must a firearm's destruction be reported?",
 "No later than 24 hours after learning","No later than 7 days after learning","No later than 30 days after learning","At the next licence renewal",
 "Loss, theft and destruction all trigger reporting within 24 hours after the person becomes aware.")
Q("Under section 106, how long does an owner not carrying the firearm have to produce it?",
 "Within seven days","Immediately","Within 24 hours","Within 30 days",
 "Subject to section 107, section 106 requires production within seven days. The rule for a firearm being carried is different.")
Q("What does section 25(2) require of the Registrar on receiving an address change notice?",
 "Acknowledge receipt in writing within 30 days","Issue a new licence card within 30 days","Inspect the new address within 60 days","Ask the local DFO to confirm it within 7 days",
 "Section 25(2) requires the Registrar to acknowledge receipt of the notice in writing within 30 days after receiving it. The Act does not require a new card or an inspection for an address change.")
Q("How quickly does section 120(2) require reporting a firearm not in lawful possession?",
 "Without delay","Within 24 hours","Within 7 days","Within 48 hours",
 "The statutory wording is without delay. The 24 hour rule is a separate duty for loss or theft of a firearm the person controlled.")
Q("A careless shot smashes a neighbour's window. Which section 120(3) offence fits?",
 "Negligent use causing damage","Discharge in a public place","Handling while intoxicated","Pointing a firearm at a person",
 "Section 120(3)(a) covers injury or property damage caused by negligent use of a firearm.")
Q("Which section 120(3) prohibition covers reckless conduct with a firearm near people when nobody is hurt?",
 "Handling likely to endanger others","Negligent use causing injury","Discharge without good reason","Failure to report a loss in 24 hours",
 "Section 120(3)(b) prohibits handling likely to endanger safety or property, or with reckless disregard for them.")
Q("A loaded rifle is left against a car near playing children. Which section 120(3) failure is this?",
 "Failing to take reasonable precautions","Negligent use causing an injury to others","Discharging it recklessly in public","Pointing it without good reason",
 "Section 120(3)(c) specifically covers failure to take reasonable precautions in a risky situation with a loaded firearm.")
Q("A person handles a firearm after several beers. Which section 120 prohibition applies?",
 "Handling it while under the influence","Negligent discharge of the firearm","Reckless endangerment of others","Supplying it to an unlicensed person",
 "Section 120(4) prohibits handling a firearm while under the influence of a substance with an intoxicating or narcotic effect.")
Q("An applicant hides a past conviction on the licence form. Which offence is this?",
 "Giving false or misleading information","Altering a licence document by hand","Using another person's licence card","Making a false entry in a register",
 "Section 120(9)(f) prohibits giving knowingly false, incorrect or misleading information in an application.")
Q("A holder changes a printed detail on the card without permission. Which offence is this?",
 "Unauthorised alteration of a document","Knowingly false application information","Failure to report a lost card","Allowing the card to wear out",
 "Unauthorised additions, alterations or erasures on competency or licensing documents are prohibited.")
Q("A buyer uses a relative's licence to acquire a firearm. Which offence is this?",
 "Using someone else's document","Altering the relative's licence card","Making a false application","Failing to carry one's own licence",
 "Section 120(9)(e) prohibits using another person's document to procure possession.")
Q("A dealer records an invented sale in its required records. Which offence is this?",
 "False entry in a required register","Dealing in firearms without a licence","Altering a licence card by hand","Reporting a firearm sale late",
 "Section 120(9)(g) prohibits making a false entry in a register required under the Act.")
Q("A person jokingly aims a realistic toy gun at a friend. Which prohibition applies?",
 "Pointing it without good reason","No offence, since it is not real","Negligent discharge","Possessing an unlicensed firearm",
 "Section 120(6) includes objects likely to be taken for firearms. A joke does not by itself give good reason.")
Q("A person fires shots into the air at a street party. Which offence applies?",
 "Discharge in public without good reason","Negligent use causing property damage","No offence, as nobody was hurt","Pointing a firearm without good reason",
 "Section 120(7) prohibits firing a firearm in a built-up area or public place without good reason.")
Q("An owner lets a visibly drunk friend hold a loaded pistol. Which prohibition applies?",
 "Giving control to an impaired person","Negligent discharge of a firearm","Supplying a prohibited firearm to him","No offence if the owner stays nearby",
 "Section 120(5) prohibits giving control of a firearm to a person known, or who should reasonably be known, to be under the influence.")
Q("A seller hands a firearm to a buyer with no lawful authority. Which offence is involved?",
 "Supplying someone not allowed to have it","Dealing in firearms without a permit","Failing to report the transfer in time","No offence, since the buyer paid for it",
 "Section 120(10)(a) prohibits giving possession to someone who is not allowed under the Act to possess the firearm.")
Q("What happens to a person's licences and certificates when a section 102 unfitness declaration is final?",
 "They cease to be valid","They stay valid until expiry","They are suspended for 60 days","Only the main licence lapses",
 "Section 104(1) makes them cease to be valid from the date of the declaration or conviction.")
Q("What deadline does section 104 set for a newly unfit person's firearms and documents?",
 "Hand them in to police within 24 hours","Hand them in within 7 days","Dispose of them within 60 days","Hand them in only after any appeal",
 "Section 104(2) requires surrender to the nearest police station within 24 hours.")
Q("After a primary holder's unfitness becomes final, how soon must a section 12 holder apply under section 104(4)?",
 "Within 30 days","Within 7 days","Within 60 days","Within 90 days",
 "Section 104(4) lets the additional licence holder apply for a new licence in their own name. If no application is made within 30 days of the time for an appeal elapsing, or of an appeal that leaves the unfitness in place being finalised, the additional licence lapses.")
Q("How may a person declared unfit sell surrendered firearms?",
 "Through a dealer or as the Registrar says","Private sale to any licensed buyer","Giving them to a licensed family member","Leaving them with police indefinitely",
 "Section 104 requires disposal through a dealer or as the Registrar determines, within the applicable period. Surrender does not allow a private handover.")
Q("Complying with section 104, a person surrenders firearms and ammunition but keeps their papers. What is omitted?",
 "All competency and licensing documents","Firearm safe from the house","Keys to the empty firearm safe","Their identity document and passport",
 "The surrender duty includes all competency certificates, licences, permits and authorisations issued to the person under the Act.")
Q("An unfit person keeps a firearm not involved in the incident. What has been omitted?",
 "Every firearm they hold","Only firearms used in the incident","Holster for that firearm","Nothing, as only the incident firearm counts",
 "The duty covers all firearms in the person's possession, not only one involved in the event.")
Q("An unfit person surrenders everything but some cartridges. What has been omitted?",
 "All remaining ammunition","Only loaded magazines","Cleaning equipment","Only rounds above 200",
 "Section 104(2) also requires surrender of all ammunition in the person's possession.")
Q("Where must a section 104 surrender be made?",
 "Nearest police station","Licensed dealer","Appeal Board","Registrar's head office",
 "Section 104 specifies the nearest police station, not a dealer or another private recipient.")
Q("Under section 107, what must a person carrying a firearm do when asked for its licence?",
 "Produce it for inspection on request","Produce it within seven days","Refuse until a lawyer is present","Show an identity document only",
 "A person carrying a firearm must produce its licence, permit or authorisation for inspection when asked. The general seven-day period does not apply.")
Q("During a section 107 check, how must the carrier respond when asked who they are?",
 "Identify themselves forthwith","Identify themselves within seven days","Show only the firearm licence","Decline unless under arrest",
 "The person must identify themselves forthwith to the official's satisfaction.")
Q("Under section 107, what must a carrier do when an official wants to examine the pistol?",
 "Hand over the firearm for inspection","Bring it to a station within seven days","Refuse, as the licence is enough","Let only a DFO inspect it",
 "The person must also produce the firearm for inspection when requested under section 107.")
Q("Which safeguard applies before someone exercises section 107 inspection powers?",
 "The official must show their authority","The person must comply regardless","Only uniformed police may make it","A court warrant must be shown first",
 "The official exercising these powers must identify themselves and produce the appointment certificate or authorisation.")
Q("The Appeal Board leaves a challenged ruling unchanged. Which outcome is this?",
 "Confirming the decision","Varying it","Reversing it","Sending it back to the DFO",
 "Section 133 allows the Board to confirm a decision.")
Q("The Appeal Board changes part of a ruling and keeps the rest. Which power is this?",
 "Varying the decision","Confirming it","Reversing it in full","Setting it aside entirely",
 "Section 133 allows the Board to vary the decision, not only to accept or reject it as a whole.")
Q("The Appeal Board overturns a challenged refusal. Which outcome is this?",
 "Reversing the decision","Confirming it","Varying part of it","Postponing it",
 "Reversing a decision is one of the powers listed in section 133.")
Q("An appeal notice arrives after the deadline with a substantiated reason. What may the Board do under regulation 91?",
 "Condone the late submission on good cause","Reject it automatically for lateness","Accept it only if the Registrar agrees","Refer it to the High Court",
 "The Board may condone late submission on good cause. Lateness is not excused merely because the person wants to appeal.")
Q("Which provision lets a court infer who possessed a firearm found on specified premises?",
 "Section 117 presumption","Section 118 presumption","Section 119 presumption","Direct eyewitness evidence",
 "Section 117 provides evidential presumptions about possession. Its conditions matter, and contrary evidence raising reasonable doubt can defeat it.")
Q("A possessor fails to produce an allegedly missing firearm within seven days of a police request. Which provision applies?",
 "Section 118, on failure to report","Section 117, on possession","Direct evidence from a witness","A conclusive rule that cannot be rebutted",
 "Section 118 provides the specified inference relating to loss, theft or destruction and failure to report. It is not irrebuttable.")
Q("Which presumption concerns failing to keep unlawful firearms off controlled premises?",
 "Section 119","Section 117","Section 140","Section 120",
 "Section 119 addresses failure to take reasonable steps, subject to its evidential qualifications.")
Q("A witness describes a theft they saw. What kind of proof is this?",
 "Direct first-hand evidence","Section 118 presumption","Section 117 presumption","Hearsay from the owner's family",
 "First-hand testimony describes the event directly. It is different from relying on a statutory presumption, though its reliability must still be assessed.")
Q("What must police give at once to a person who informs them face to face under section 120(2)?",
 "Written proof that the report was made","Case number for the report sent later","Nothing until an investigation opens","Copy of the officer's notebook",
 "For such a report, section 120(2)(b) requires written proof that the report was made. A reference number is used for telephonic or similar reports.")
Q("What acknowledgement does the Act specify for a section 120(2) report by telephone?",
 "Official reference number","Written receipt posted within 7 days","Nothing, as phone reports are informal","Court docket number",
 "Section 120(2)(b) requires the official to give written proof of the report or, for a telephonic or similar report, the official reference number.")
Q("A firearm is stolen in another town. Under section 120(11), which station gets the report?",
 "The one nearest to where it was stolen","One nearest to the owner's home","The one that processed the licence","Any station within 30 days",
 "The statute specifies the police station nearest to the place where the loss, theft or destruction occurred.")
Q("What starts the section 120(11) reporting period for a theft found next morning?",
 "When the possessor became aware of it","Estimated time of the theft","When the insurer is told about it","Midnight on the day it happened",
 "The 24 hour period runs from becoming aware of the loss, theft or destruction, not from an unknown time when it happened.")
Q("Under section 148, how may an heir keep an inherited firearm usable?",
 "Applying for the right licence or permit","Relying on the will as authority","Re-registering it in the estate's name","Continuing under the deceased's licence",
 "The heir must apply for the appropriate licence, permit or authorisation. Inheritance is not a licence to possess.")
Q("Under section 148, how may an heir keep an inherited firearm as an ornament?",
 "Lawful deactivation under the Act","Removing the firing pin themselves","Keeping it under the deceased's licence","Storing it unloaded indefinitely",
 "Section 148 allows deactivation in accordance with the Act. This is a regulated process, not permission for improvised alteration.")
Q("An heir would rather pass on an inherited firearm than deactivate it. Which option applies?",
 "Dispose of it in terms of the Act","Give it to anyone who wants it","Hand it to a relative to keep","Sell it privately with a receipt",
 "The firearm must be disposed of in terms of the Act, so both the recipient and the transfer route must be lawful.")
Q("Which section 148(2) route gives an heir time-limited permission while arranging disposal?",
 "Section 21 temporary authorisation","Section 13 licence","Dealer's licence","Regulation 86(4) storage authorisation",
 "The Registrar may issue a section 21 temporary authorisation to give an heir reasonable time to dispose of the firearm.")
Q("An unauthorised person walks into a declared firearm-free stadium with a holstered pistol. What is breached?",
 "Ban on bringing firearms into the zone","Nothing, as a licence overrides the zone notice","Ban on storing ammunition there","Only a rule against discharging it",
 "A normal licence does not override the prohibition on carrying firearms or ammunition into a declared zone without applicable authorisation.")
Q("An unauthorised person keeps cartridges in a locker in a firearm-free zone. Which section 140 restriction applies?",
 "Ban on storing ammunition there","None, since it is only ammunition","Ban on carrying a firearm in","Only the venue's own house rules",
 "Section 140 prohibits storing firearms or ammunition in the zone without applicable authorisation.")
Q("A venue manager knowingly lets guests bring firearms into a firearm-free zone. Which section 140 restriction applies?",
 "The ban on allowing firearms to be present","None, as the manager carries nothing","Only the rule on discharging firearms","Rule on reporting any firearms found",
 "Section 140 separately prohibits allowing firearms or ammunition to be in the zone without applicable authorisation.")
Q("Police find a firearm in a firearm-free zone in breach of the notice. Which section 140 power lets them take it?",
 "Seizure of the firearm","Seven-day production notice","Cancelling the licence on the spot","Declaring the person unfit",
 "Section 140 permits seizure of firearms or ammunition present in breach of the zone notice, subject to the statutory conditions.")
assert len(ITEMS)==52
